-overgrowing layer of 2 that is terminated when it reaches the $1.4\:\:\mathsf{n m}$ high terrace of 1 as schematically represented in Figure 2e. Subsequent layers of 2 can grow over these 0.45 nm step height mismatches to allow continuous layer spreading of 2 over the {0001} faces of 1. The nuclei shown in Figure 2d IV and V demonstrate that the layers of 2 can overgrow terrace height mismatches of $0.45\ \mathsf{n m}$ with movement up (Fig 2d IV) or down (Fig 2d V) and over such a height mismatch. For the latter this requires an additional nonovergrowing layer of 2 to grow that is terminated when it reaches the $1.4\ \mathsf{n m}$ high terrace of 1 and provides some indication that the rate of nucleation of a nucleus of 2 on 2 is similar to the rate of terrace overgrowth across the height mismatch.
These $0.45\ \mathsf{n m}$ terrace height mismatches are found to persist within the shell of 2 as it grows in thickness as displayed by the $0.45~\mathsf{n m}$ high growth islands in Figures 3 and S8 - S9 that display the in situ growth of 2 over 1 monitored for a much longer time than that shown in Figure 2. Figures 3 and $S8\mathrm{~-~}59$ also demonstrate that layers of 2 can completely overgrow these $0.45\ \mathsf{n m}$ high terrace mismatched features corroborating the mechanism of growth proposed for the growing nuclei of 2 over the $0.45~\mathsf{n m}$ height mismatches. Also evidenced in Figure 3 is the presence of 0.45 nm high mismatched hexagonal growth islands (marked C and D in Figure 3) around which the developing $1.0\ \mathsf{n m}$ layer of 2 cannot overgrow. The approximately $30^{\circ}$ rotation of these hexagonal growth islands with respect to the bulk crystal orientation shown in Figure S10 suggests the existence of domains of 2 or 1 that are structurally misaligned in the (0001) plane with that of the new layer of 2 that may try but is prevented from overgrowing it. This type of defect has not been reported for a MOF but is known to occur on a much smaller scale in hexagonal nanoporous materials such as zeolite L.[31]
The growth mechanism of 2 introduces many framework Zn atoms that will be undercoordinated by the framework organic linkers at the locations of the $0.45~\mathsf{n m}$ terrace height mismatches at the interface of 1 and 2 as represented by the red $Z\mathsf{n}_{2}$ nodes in Figure 2e
Attempts at in situ growth of a shell of 2 on a {1010} face of 1 were expected to be unsuccessful due to the $\sim32\%$ mismatch of the c-parameters and thus lattice/ structure mismatching of 1 and 2 in the ac-plane which has resulted in anisotropic crystal growth only in a structurally related MOF,[12] although ${\sim}10\mathrm{~-~}20\%$ lattice mismatch has been present in the formation of crystal films of multilayers of structurally related 2-dimensional MOFs.[15, 16] However, rapid in situ growth of a shell of 2 was observed on a {1010} face of 1 when the pure DMF surrounding the core 1 crystal was replaced by a low supersaturation growth solution of 2 as shown in Figures 4, S11 – S12. The difference in crystal form of a {1010} surface of 1 and shell 2 is highlighted in Figure 4a and b where approximately one rectangular growth hillock dominates the image with step heights of $1.9\ \mathsf{n m}$ in Figure 4a (see Figure S13a) that is replaced by numerous approximately rectangular growth islands covering the same area in Figure 4b also with step heights of $1.9\ \mathsf{n m}$ (see Figure S13b). The orientation of the rectangular growth terraces in Figure 4a and 4b (see also Figures S11 - S12) along a ${<}0001>$ direction suggests epitaxial growth of 2 on 1. Additional evidence that 2 has grown is provided by the Raman spectrum collected from a ${\sim}858\ \mathsf{n m}$ diameter sampling spot size focussed on a {1010} and {0001} surface of the CS-LM-MOF that both show additional dabco related peaks in the range $2840\textrm{-}3000\textrm{c m}^{-1}$ due to the $-C H_{2}$ - stretching vibrations that are absent in the spectrum of 1 as seen in Figure S14.
